The text defines four methodological categories in the scientific study of medicine:

• Observation: what we learn by using our senses;
• Experiment: something done to discover an unknown effect;
• Induction: generalizing from observation and experiment;
• Deduction: deriving consequences from premised principles.

Suppose a medical researcher wants to evaluate a novel alkaloid extracted from a plant for its efficacy in reducing fever. Drawing exclusively on these definitions and on the author’s account of medical methodology, design a step-by-step research protocol. For each step, specify: (1) which methodological category it exemplifies; (2) how it adheres to the author’s insistence on the primacy of induction in medical science; and (3) what safeguards or checks ensure against false deductions. Your answer should demonstrate the logical structure of an inductive research program in medicine, as characterized in the text.

The text describes a proposal to modernize the medical curriculum by allowing certain foundational medical courses—chemistry, anatomy, physiology, and botany—to count toward both the Bachelor of Arts and the Doctor of Medicine degrees. The majority report at Harvard College objects that this proposal “abolishes the fundamental distinction between liberal and professional studies” and “confounds all distinction between pure and applied science.”

Using only the arguments and definitions provided in the text, critically analyze the merits and potential pitfalls of this proposal. Specifically:
(1) Identify and articulate the underlying assumptions on both sides regarding what constitutes a “liberal” versus a “professional” study;
(2) Evaluate whether the medical courses in question satisfy the author’s criteria for a liberal education;
(3) Discuss, based on the text’s characterization of pure and applied science, whether this integration genuinely blurs meaningful distinctions or simply reflects the author’s broader conception of liberal learning.

Ro names its digits 1–9 and 0 using the ten consonants b, c, d, f, g, l, m, q, r, x (in alphabetical order) each preceded by the prefix za- to form pronounceable numbers: zab (1), zac (2), zad (3), … zar (9), zax (0). Similarly, to denote time units it uses the prefix ta-: tab (second), tac (minute), … taf (day), etc., and by adding a consonant (b, c, d, etc.) to taf (day) it derives days of the week: tafb (Sunday), tafc (Monday), etc.  

Evaluate the scalability, consistency, and cognitive ergonomics of this numerical and temporal naming scheme for:  
1) extending to larger magnitudes (hundreds, thousands, fractional units);  
2) integrating compound numbers (e.g., 23, 507);  
3) handling irregular time units (weeks, months, years);  
4) avoiding homonyms with existing Ra roots (e.g., b-words meaning substances, f-words meaning form).  

Propose a general, systematic set of rules or layer of affixation—drawing only on Ro’s existing mechanisms—to reliably generate all higher-order number names and temporal measures without collision.

An occupying authority (OA) is confronting a domestic political party (DP) that has shifted from seeking parliamentary influence to emphasizing extra-legal opposition, including calls for a “general strike,” with the explicit aim of portraying OA as an imperialistic, repressive force acting against the interests of the local population. DP’s propaganda magnifies a relatively minor incident—assault of a few OA military personnel by DP supporters—into evidence of systemic oppression. An OA spokesman publicly declares that DP’s foreign controllers are now exposed and warns that OA will “crack down hard” on DP activities. 

Based solely on this scenario:
1. Analyze why the spokesman’s “crack down hard” message plays into DP’s imperialism narrative and may worsen polarization among the local population.
2. Devise an alternative OA public communication strategy that:
a) Clearly states the legal framework governing both OA and the local government;
b) Frames DP illegal tactics as comparable to common criminal activity;
c) Upholds OA’s duty to enforce these laws without invoking “imperialism” rhetoric;
d) Minimizes DP’s ability to depict OA as acting against local interests.

Provide detailed reasoning for each element of your alternative strategy.

