(a) Definition of N(r):

We let r denote the citizen’s rental income (in Lira). The text describes two regimes:

• If r ≤ 400, the tax on mobile wealth is zero. Hence net income N(r) = r.

• If r ≥ 401, the tax is L. 33.75 flat, so net income N(r) = r – 33.75.

We can capture this as a piecewise function, noting that the text describes a jump at r = 401. We assume that for 400 < r < 401 (i.e. non-integer incomes) either the same rule applies or the author focuses on whole Lira. For simplicity, we treat it as:

N(r) = {
  r,                          if 0 ≤ r ≤ 400;
  r – 33.75,             if r ≥ 401.
}

Graphically, this function has:

• A straight line of slope 1 from (0,0) to (400,400).

• A parallel line of slope 1, but 33.75 units lower, starting at (401, 401–33.75 = 367.25) and extending to infinity.

(b) Identification of the paradox:

The paradox arises because at r = 400, net income N(400) = 400, but at r = 401, N(401) = 401 – 33.75 = 367.25. Thus:

N(401) = 367.25 < N(400) = 400.

This violates the basic fairness principle that higher gross income should never yield lower net income after tax (i.e., N(r) ought to be nondecreasing). Formally, there is a discontinuity and a drop of 32.75 Lira at the threshold. Even worse, the critic points out that since 400 Lira was deemed minimal for subsistence, any net below 400 is inconsistent with the legislature’s own criterion, producing an absurd result right at the threshold.

(c) Proposal of a corrected piecewise function N*(r):

To honor the intention:
• incomes up to 400 must be fully exempt,
• ensure that N*(r) is nondecreasing (monotonic),
• avoid creating a net income below 400 at the threshold,
• and still impose a tax for incomes above the exemption.

A common mathematical remedy is to introduce a marginal tax rather than a lump sum. For example, let us impose a constant marginal rate t on the income exceeding 400. That is:

N*(r) = {
  r,                          if 0 ≤ r ≤ 400;
  400 + (1 – t)·(r – 400),   if r > 400,
}

where 0 < t < 1 is chosen so that at some point the total tax revenue grows appropriately. This design ensures:

1. Continuity at r = 400:
   N*(400) = 400;
   limit as r→400+ of N*(r) = 400 + (1 – t)·(400 – 400) = 400.

2. Monotonicity everywhere, since both segments have nonnegative slope:
   • For r ≤ 400, slope = 1;
   • For r > 400, slope = 1 – t (positive but less than 1).

3. Net incomes for r > 400 always exceed 400, preserving subsistence:
   For any r > 400, N*(r) = 400 + (1 – t)(r – 400) > 400.

To calibrate t so that the initial marginal “bite” resembles the critic’s 33.75 lump sum at r=401, one could set:

(1 – t)·(401 – 400) = 401 – 33.75 – 400 = –66.75

so 1 – t = 367.25, which is impossible because 1–t must lie in (0,1). This shows that a pure marginal rate cannot replicate the flat deduction in the critic’s example for a 1-lira increase. But the goal is not to mimic the exact L. 33.75 lump sum, but to preserve fairness.

Alternatively, one could design a hybrid:

N*(r) = {
  r,                                   if r ≤ 400;
  r – 33.75 + k·max(0, 400 – (r – 33.75)),   if r > 400;
}

where k ∈ [0,1] is a smoothing parameter that restores the shortfall at the threshold. Specifically, define the shortfall S(r) = max(0, 400 – (r – 33.75)). Then adding k·S(r) ensures that at r just above 400, the net income cannot drop below 400. For instance, at r = 401:

Raw net = 401 – 33.75 = 367.25;
Shortfall S(401) = 400 – 367.25 = 32.75;
Choose k = 1 to fully offset the shortfall:
N*(401) = 367.25 + 1·32.75 = 400.

Thus the corrected function is:

N*(r) = {
  r,                               if r ≤ 400;
  (r – 33.75) + max(0, 400 – (r – 33.75)),   if r > 400.
}

This ensures:

• Continuity at r = 400: both pieces equal 400;
• Nondecreasing net income: any increase in r either raises r–33.75 or reduces the shortfall term, never lowering N*(r);
• No net income below 400 for r > 400.

Summary of justification:

By smoothing out the discontinuity with a corrective "shortfall offset," we preserve the legislature’s desire to tax incomes above 400 while avoiding the perverse consequence that a slightly higher income yields less net purchasing power than the subsistence threshold. The revised function N*(r) maintains fairness (monotonicity) and respects the subsistence principle (net ≥ 400), solving the critic’s paradox without discounting the tax’s revenue goal.

