In a certain canton, a collective fund known as the “Ministerialfonds” has existed since the Reformation (circa 1536). Its revenues have for centuries been used solely to satisfy the common economic needs of all clergy serving in that canton. This fund was never merged into the general state treasury; rather, its assets have always been administered separately and applied exclusively to clerical purposes, under various historical arrangements recognized by the state government (including contracts of 1654, early-19th-century facility-exchange agreements, and a detailed 1837 deed).

The canton’s private-law order recognizes three categories of “juristische Personen” (legal persons) distinct from natural persons:
  a. Personengesamtheiten (corporations) – associations of persons whose identity as a legal unit persists despite membership changes;
  b. Anstalten (institutions) – enduring public or private establishments created for a lawful permanent purpose, having organizational elements (e.g. hospitals, schools, savings banks), but not constituted by membership;
  c. Vermögensinbegriffe (aggregates of assets endowed with personality) – enduring asset masses which, without belonging to any natural or juridical owner, possess legal capacity. These come in two subtypes:
     • c1. Asset aggregates intended to await an eventual human or corporate owner (e.g. a “lying inheritance”);
     • c2. Dedicated funds whose yields are destined directly for the benefit of a defined (often limited) group of persons (e.g. family endowments, scholarship foundations).

Using only these definitions and the described historical facts, determine under which category the Ministerialfonds must be classified. Then analyze whether, under that classification, it constitutes its own legal subject capable of holding property rights and entering valid contracts with the state, independently of the individual clergy members.

The advisory opinion acknowledges a common objection to voluntary prayer sessions before and after school: that children exempted by their parents may experience feelings of exclusion or inferiority if required to wait outside the school during the prayer, or to be released from class before prayer begins. The advisory rebuts this objection by analogy to voluntary religious instruction, arguing that:

• Non-attendance at voluntary religious instruction is accepted as a normal parental choice without causing unconstitutional exclusion. 
• Similarly, non-attendance at voluntary prayer cannot constitute an exclusionary practice.

Using only the information provided above, design a methodologically rigorous social science study to empirically test whether exempted students actually experience feelings of exclusion when voluntary prayer sessions are held before and after regular school hours. Your proposal should include:
   a) A clear hypothesis;
   b) Identification of key variables (independent, dependent, control variables);
   c) A detailed description of the sampling strategy;
   d) Proposed data collection methods and instruments;
   e) An outline of the analysis plan;
   f) Consideration of potential confounds and how to address them.

Context: Under the Trade Expansion Act of 1962 as described, the President has four distinct types of new tariff‐reduction authorities: (1) a basic authority to reduce any duty existing on July 1, 1962, by up to 50 percent in bilateral negotiations; (2) special authority vis-à-vis the European Economic Community (EEC) to reduce industrial‐product duties by more than 50 percent or eliminate them altogether if the United States and the EEC together account for at least 80 percent of the world export value in the relevant statistical category, and to reduce or eliminate agricultural‐product duties if it “tends to assure the maintenance or expansion of U.S. exports of like articles”; (3) special authority regarding tropical agricultural and forestry commodities not produced in significant quantities in the United States, conditioned on a finding that the EEC has taken comparable action; and (4) authority to eliminate low duties of 5 percent ad valorem or less.

Task:  Using only the criteria and constraints specified in this Act, design a precise decision-making algorithm (in prose or pseudocode) that the President could follow to determine, for any given tariff line on an imported article, which of the four authorities to invoke.  Your algorithm must
•  incorporate all four authorities and their conditions;  
•  ensure that the President never exceeds the permissible reduction/elimination under each authority;  
•  explicitly handle cases where multiple authorities appear applicable (for example, a low-duty tropical product also covered by the 80 percent EEC rule);  
•  respect the Act’s requirement that any reduction be advised by the Tariff Commission, and that no items under escape-clause or national-security reserve be negotiated;  
•  guarantee that the most liberal permissible concession is identified without violating any statutory preclusion.

Provide the algorithm in clear numbered steps or well–structured pseudocode.  Explain how your algorithm enforces every statutory constraint and how conflicts are resolved when more than one authority could apply.

A critique is leveled against narrow statutory formulations of legitimate defense that restrict its scope to violent aggression against the person. Drawing on the following synthesized arguments:

• Legitimate defense is not confined to protection of bodily integrity—it must extend to defense of the entire legal person and all its associated rights, including property.

• If social force (i.e., police or civil authority) cannot instantaneously intervene, the individual is entitled to self-help to repel illegal aggression that is imminent and inevitable, provided she did not provoke it.

• Various foreign codes (e.g., Bavarian, Spanish, Brazilian, Portuguese, English) recognize a broad concept of “incolpata tutela” or “collisione giuridica” encompassing property defense, non-provocation, and authorization for reasonable force.

Based only on this critique, reconstruct a comprehensive statutory definition of legitimate defense that:

(a) Incorporates both personal and property rights;
(b) Requires the aggression to be unlawful, imminent, and inevitable;
(c) Mandates absence of prior provocation by the defender;
(d) Specifies that force employed must be proportionate and not exceed what is necessary, even if in reaction to terror or shock;
(e) Is suitable for comprehension by lay jurors.

Additionally, outline the three core reasons presented for preferring the Zurich Code’s wording on this subject.

Legislatures considering how to regulate false oaths in civil proceedings have proposed or enacted three distinct models:  
  (1) Total abolition of perjury as a criminal offense when the oath is given by a party in civil litigation;  
  (2) Unrestricted criminal sanction for any false oath by a party in civil litigation;  
  (3) Conditional sanction: perjury is punishable only when the charge is supported by a decisive written document that did not exist or could not be produced before the oath.  
Critically evaluate the advantages and drawbacks of each model with respect to three goals: (a) preserving the finality and efficiency of civil proof procedures, (b) deterring false oaths and protecting judicial integrity, and (c) maintaining coherence and non‐conflict between civil and criminal law. Which model strikes the most balanced compromise, and why? Provide a reasoned argument drawing exclusively on principles and policy considerations relevant to the regulation of decisive oaths in civil and criminal contexts.

