Pseudocode Decision Procedure:

Input:
  claimed_harm       // Boolean
  causal_link        // Boolean
  filed_complaint    // Boolean
  creditor_of_victim // Boolean

Output:
  decision ∈ {YES, NO}

Procedure determine_civil_party_eligibility():
  // Step 1: Check if applicant claims patrimonial loss
  if not claimed_harm then
    // No damage claimed → no standing
    return NO  // Justification: Standing requires patrimonial loss (Rule 3)
  end if

  // Step 2: Check causal link between crime and loss
  if not causal_link then
    // Loss not directly caused by crime → no standing
    return NO  // Justification: Damage must be direct (Rule 3)
  end if

  // Step 3: At this point, applicant is an injured party
  // per definition of damage and causal link.

  // Step 4: Any injured party may constitute, complaint optional
  if filed_complaint or (not filed_complaint) then
    // Under Rule 1, complaint status immaterial
    decision ← YES  // Justification: Rule 1 allows civil party even without complaint
    return decision
  end if

  // Redundant—but shows explicit logic

  // Step 5: Alternatively, if applicant is a creditor of the victim,
  // they may exercise the victim’s rights (Rule 4)
  if creditor_of_victim then
    return YES  // Justification: Rule 4 confers all rights of victim, including standing
  end if

  // Fallback—should not be reached
  return NO
end procedure

Explanation of Nodes and Justifications:

• Node “claimed_harm == false → NO”: A party must claim a patrimonial loss (Rules 1 & 3).
• Node “causal_link == false → NO”: The loss must be directly caused by the crime (Rule 3).
• Node after causal_link: Applicant is a legally recognized injured party.
• Node “filed_complaint or not filed_complaint → YES”: Complaint is immaterial (Rule 1).
• Node “creditor_of_victim == true → YES”: Creditors stand in place of the victim (Rule 4).

Since Rules 1–4 impose no further restrictions, any party meeting the harm + causal link test, or any creditor thereof, is eligible.

qa_pairs.question:
Consider a penological framework in which punishment aims primarily at the protection of society, yet must also account for retribution (or expiation). One schematic formulation reads: “just punishment is necessary punishment,” where necessity is judged by reference to protective effects rather than metaphysical notions of desert. Criminals are classified into three types: 1) habitual uncorrectable offenders, 2) habitual correctable offenders, and 3) occasional offenders. For type (1), the proposal is: (a) impose an ordinary sentence for the last offence; (b) place the offender under special surveillance for a fixed period; (c) if risk of new offences remains, convert to an indeterminate (initially perpetual) sentence, revocable only upon exceptional circumstances reported periodically by the prison administration back to the sentencing judge.

Critically evaluate the coherence and practicability of this two-stage procedure for habitual uncorrectable offenders. In your answer:
– Summarize the normative rationale linking protection to indeterminate detention;
– Identify at least three epistemic or procedural challenges this scheme faces when transferred from theory to judicial practice;
– Assess whether periodic prison reports to the sentencing judge can realistically supply the necessary grounds for revocation decisions without introducing unjust arbitrariness or excessive trial-like burdens over already-sentenced individuals.

A penal theorist distinguishes sharply between the tasks of the legislature and those of the judge. The legislature must define offences in general, set proportional maximum penalties for each crime, and establish special minima only for grave circumstances the law specifically foresees. The judge, studying each case’s unique facts—objective harms, subjective intent, collateral social consequences—must then set a sentence freely within the legislative maximum, and may even descend below statutory minima in light of unforeseeable mitigating factors. 

Analyze the normative and practical coherence of this division of labor. Specifically:
1. Explain why legislative specification of maximum penalties but abolition of special minima could facilitate better individualized sentencing;  
2. Identify potential risks or drawbacks of empowering judges to impose sentences below ordinary statutory minima based on unscripted mitigating circumstances;  
3. Evaluate whether removing special legislative minima necessarily makes legislative aggravating or qualifying provisions obsolete, or if they retain distinct justificatory roles in the overall sentencing framework.

Context:
A forensic team concludes that physiological testing of aqueous/hydroalcoholic cadaveric extracts on live animals cannot reliably prove exogenous poisoning, because endogenous tissue extracts themselves are toxic.  They generalize that the “physiological criterion” for detecting organic poisons should be abandoned except for highly purified ether extracts.  Their inference rests on:  
– Similar paralytic toxicity in frogs and general toxicity in dogs produced by both endogenous extracts and reference meat extract;
– Absence of soporific or respiratory-depressant signs (characteristic of opium) in cadaver extracts;
– Non-toxicity of purified ether fractions from non-poisoned cadavers.

Critically analyze the logical structure of this conclusion.  Identify and discuss two potential weaknesses or unstated assumptions in the argument that could limit the validity of abandoning physiological testing for organic poisons.

Under the existing criminal‐procedure framework, a collegial body (Camera di Consiglio) conducts a secret preliminary hearing to decide both on a suspect’s pretrial liberty and on whether the case should proceed to trial.  A reform proposal would abolish the two‐tier collegial preliminary decision, replacing it with:

  1. A single-tier collegial ‘Chamber of Oppositions’ or Section of Accusation, composed of a higher‐ranking magistrate body;
  2. A written indictment by the Public Prosecutor, publicly filed;
  3. An automatic route to trial absent any defendant opposition within a short deadline;
  4. If opposition is filed, a written contestation and brief submissions by both sides, decided by the new collegial body.

Reconstruct the commission’s reasoning for (a) eliminating the separate Camera di Consiglio and single‐magistrate preliminary hearing, (b) introducing a written indictment plus automatic trial route, and (c) vesting the new collegial body with both liberty and admissibility decisions.  Then evaluate, within the logic of their proposal, whether the combination of public indictment and limited right of opposition effectively balances speedy trial with safeguards against wrongful prosecutions.

In the historical practice of 16th–18th-century Ottoman capitulations, European consuls in Ottoman ports exercised jurisdiction only over their own nationals, following the principle actor sequitur forum rei: the plaintiff must file in the forum of the defendant’s nationality. Over time, consular jurisdiction expanded by tacit mutual arrangement to cover cases where a European plaintiff sued a non-European defendant—effectively granting tribunals extraterritorial jurisdiction over all foreign parties. Drawing exclusively on the following principles and precedents:

• The original consular jurisdiction rested upon grants in 9th–10th-century Arabia and early 12th-century Egypt, allowing dispute resolution by a kadi or podestat of the merchants’ own nationality;

• Subsequent Ottoman capitulations (1535, 1604, renewals through 1740) confirmed that consuls “should and may hear, judge, and determine . . . both civil and criminal cases arising between merchants and other subjects” of the consul’s sovereign;

• Grotius and Martens both recognize that a host state may protest and refuse enforcement when a foreign tribunal exceeds its treaty bounds;

craft an analytical response to this situation:

A European consul today asserts jurisdiction to try a civil case where a European national sues a Japanese defendant in Tokyo, under a treaty allowing the consul “to hear, judge, and determine both civil and criminal cases arising between” any subjects of his sovereign. The Japanese government objects, claiming: (i) the extension to plaintiffs is outside the “original” reciprocal understanding of consular jurisdiction; (ii) the treaty grants jurisdiction only over the consul’s own nationals. 

Using only the above principles and precedents, evaluate:

1. Whether the consul’s assertion of jurisdiction in favor of a European plaintiff over a Japanese defendant is valid under the expanded mutual practice of capitulations; and

2. What remedies the Japanese government may employ under the law of nations if the consul delivers a judgment and seeks local enforcement.

A newly formed federal republic has inherited substantial debts contracted by its provinces.  

  •  Province A owes 40 million on foreign debt and 47 million on internal debt;  
  •  The federal state owes 300 million of external debt on its own account, plus additional contingent liabilities for provinces.  

  Province after province contracted bank loans and external bonds under a national law that encouraged issuance by guaranteeing province-issued paper with federal funds.  Provincial budgets have ballooned—several now spend more than twice their ordinary revenues—and their combined internal and external debt nearly equals the federal debt.  75 % of provincial budgets is devoted to debt service, versus 28 % at the federal level.  Their note-issuing banks are now insolvent, have ceased payments, and bear forged or unpaid public‐deposit securities.  

  The federal government now considers assuming all provincial debts in exchange for:  
  1) Provinces ceding ownership of any banks or public works built with those loan proceeds;  
  2) A single conversion of provincial bonds into federal external‐debt instruments at 4½ % interest, 1 % cumulative amortization;  
  3) Mandatory assignment by each province of specific ordinary‐revenue streams dedicated to unified debt service;  
  4) Absolute prohibition on any future province borrowing.  

  Critically assess the advantages and risks of this debt-assumption scheme.  Identify at least two potential legal, fiscal or political obstacles to its implementation, and propose refinements to strengthen its feasibility and equity.

Between 1887 and 1889, an Argentine federation’s Congress approved extraordinary federal outlays for provincial projects totaling more than three times the annual ordinary revenues of some small provinces, while simultaneously collecting modest national ‘subventions’ for primary education (ca. 1 600 000 pesos per year) and strictly auditing their use by local authorities. Yet the same legislature voted 265 000 pesos for bridges in Province X (whose ordinary budget was 80 000), 1 070 000 pesos for irrigation works in Province Y (budget 319 000), and hundreds of thousands more for cathedrals, penal institutions, canals, and agriculture schools in various provinces.  

  Design a regulatory framework for federal subsidies and extraordinary appropriations that:  
  a) Ensures equity among provinces with disparate resource capacities;  
  b) Applies transparent criteria for need and project selection;  
  c) Prevents political favoritism and clientelism in budgetary votes.  

  Your proposal must be self-contained and stem only from insights suggested by these episodes of over-and under-spending.

J. Hittorf’s method of analyzing electrolyte conduction (section 3) divides a tube of electrolyte solution into several compartments, passes a known electric charge through the tube for a fixed time, and then chemically assayed the change in concentration in each compartment to determine separately the transport of cations and anions.  In dilute aqueous CuSO₄, it was found that, under the same current and time, the sulfate anion travels nearly twice the distance of the Cu²⁺ cation in the opposite direction.

Design, in operational detail, an experiment using the Hittorf technique to measure separately the electrophoretic mobilities and transport numbers of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ in aqueous NaCl at three concentrations m₁, m₂, m₃.  Your design must specify:

1. The geometry and arrangement of compartments (e.g., three‐section tube), electrodes, and electrodes’ materials;
2. The procedure for passing charge and sampling compartments before and after electrolysis;
3. The chemical analytical steps to determine NaCl concentration changes in each compartment;
4. The calculations by which you would extract the individual ionic migration distances (or velocities) and transport numbers from the measured concentration changes;
5. How you would expect the results (mobilities, transport numbers) to vary with m, and what this variation would imply about ion–ion interactions and solvation effects.

Field conditions sometimes demand improvised dynamo-electric fuzes using readily available materials.  Four plans described are:
  1. Bradford’s plumbago-bridge in a wooden plug with rifle-powder charge;  
  2. Barber’s small-composition paper cap with two copper wires embedded in cork;  
  3. Moore’s pencil-lead bridge held by twisted copper wires in a wooden plug;  
  4. Pillsbury’s tin-foil bridge formed on a wooden plug with copper leads.  
Compare these four designs in terms of: (a) achievable resistance uniformity, (b) ignition delay (speed of flame initiation), (c) water-proofing and marine durability, and (d) ease and speed of manufacture under stress.  Which design would you select for immediate deployment on a naval mission, and why?

Scholar A has argued that any municipal community which supplied troops to the equites singulares (the emperor’s mounted bodyguard) must have held no more than Latin or peregrine rights, because these corps were composed exclusively of peregrini or latini, not Roman citizens. Scholar B disputes this by examining two case studies:

1. Claudia Ara (Colonia Agrippinensis), which Tacitus describes as founded ‘as a colony of veterans’, implying full Roman citizenship. 
2. Sarmizegetusa in Dacia, where an inscription of Trajan’s founding records settlement of veterans, and later jurists in the 3rd century refer to it as a colony of the ius italicum.

Scholar B contends that both communities from their foundation enjoyed Roman citizenship, so their appearance among the birthplaces of equites singulares does not support A’s rule.

Critically assess the strengths and weaknesses of Scholar B’s refutation of these two examples. In your answer, address:
• The reliability and dating of literary testimony (Tacitus) vs. inscriptions of foundation;
• The problem of inferring legal status from terminology like ‘ius italicum’ in later juristic texts;
• The chronological gap between Trajan’s founding inscription and the 3rd-century juristic reference;
• The possibility that legal status of a community evolved over time;
• Whether B adequately demonstrates that the examples fail to support A’s general rule.

Suggest how one might construct a more rigorous evidential framework to test whether providing equites singulares reliably implies Latin rather than Roman rights.

In epigraphic studies of auxiliary troops of the Roman Empire, soldiers’ tombstones and military diplomas often include an indication of place of origin, using terms like domo or natione followed by a town name in the genitive. A prevailing interpretation treats such origin markers as evidence of the legal status (Roman citizen vs. Latin vs. peregrine) of the soldier’s home community. Scholar B argues that these labels function primarily for personal identification—many non-citizens lived in Roman citizen colonies, and small satellite villages were legally bound to larger communities—so origin markers do not reliably indicate municipal legal status.

You have access to a digital corpus of 1,200 inscriptions naming auxiliary soldiers, each with: soldier’s adopted Roman name, origin formula (if present), findspot region, and secure archaeological date. Devise a research design using quantitative methods to test Scholar B’s hypothesis that origin markers on military inscriptions correlate poorly with independent indicators of municipal legal status.

In your answer, specify:
• The variables you would extract or code from each inscription;  
• How you would determine the independent legal status of each named community (e.g., roman colony, municipium, Latin town, peregrine vicus);  
• Your sampling or grouping strategy to control for chronological and regional biases;  
• The statistical test(s) or model(s) you would apply to assess the strength of association between presence/type of origin marker and municipal legal status;  
• Criteria for interpreting the results in terms of supporting or refuting B’s hypothesis.

The author argues that living folk-lore traditions provide "the key to ancient mythology," citing the widespread custom of dog-licking for healing wounds and the Armenian myth of Aralêz—supernatural dog-descended beings who lick warriors’ wounds back to life—preserved into late antiquity. Construct a rigorous methodological approach for reconstructing possible mythic narratives about healing dogs in ancient Greek religion using only these types of sources (inscriptions, ethnographic parallels, classical texts). Your answer should:
1) Define clear criteria for selecting and validating relevant data points;
2) Outline steps to distinguish between original mythic motifs and later folklore accretions;
3) Propose analytical tools for handling fragmentary or divergent accounts;
4) Address how to avoid circular reasoning when using folk-lore to explain myth and vice versa.

The text presents multiple instances of animal-based therapeutic beliefs: dogs licking wounds (Epidaurus, Citium, India, Venice, Portugal, Scotland, Bohemia, Brittany, Jamaica), contrasted with cat-licking deemed harmful. Propose a taxonomy to classify these animal-derived healing practices using only the provided examples. Your taxonomy should:
1) Define at least three orthogonal classification dimensions;
2) Assign each cited case to your taxonomy cells;
3) Demonstrate, via logical clustering (e.g., hypothetical distance metrics), how these examples group into meaningful categories;
4) Based solely on your taxonomy and clustering, infer whether these healing beliefs more likely spread by cultural diffusion or arose independently multiple times. Explain your reasoning.

A given commodity Z has highly perishable characteristics and is indispensable for daily survival, so its demand is virtually inelastic.  According to the broad principle that perishability intensifies price fluctuations under both scarcity and excess, coupled with the rule that necessary inelastic goods exhibit price changes in excess of supply variations, analyze rigorously:

(a) Why perishable necessity goods show sharper price rises during shortages than durable staples;
(b) How the same perishability affects the price decline when supply suddenly becomes abundant;
(c) The resulting implications for producers’ revenue stability and labor demand throughout the cycle.

Your answer should unpack the underlying mechanisms without invoking terms or ideas not contained in the above-stated principles.

A power initially occupied an archipelago as a temporary measure during a war, declaring solemnly in its parliament that it would ‘not impose its yoke on any civilized people.’ Decades later, it ratified a secret treaty clause stating, “It shall obtain possession of these islands,” and in formal treaties it stipulated that its occupation should become permanent. Meanwhile, it enacted decrees dissolving local representative bodies, exiling delegates, forbidding the display of national symbols, and threatening starvation by cutting food supplies unless locals renounced desires for union with their culturally aligned nation. Yet the occupied population repeatedly declared and demonstrated its will to unite with that nation, sent volunteers to fight in its army, and risked exile and imprisonment to express that will.

Evaluate the logical coherence of this occupying power’s policy shift from a declared temporary protector to an aspiring annexer. In your analysis, identify any contradictions between: 
1) the initial parliamentary promise and subsequent secret treaty clause; 
2) the principle—cited by pan-European doctrine—that a land freed from one imperial yoke should not be re-subjected, and the occupier’s treaty commitment to surrender the islands back to a former empire if certain conditions were met; 
3) the occupier’s self-professed liberal traditions and its actual implementation of repressive domestic decrees. 

Provide a structured critique pinpointing each tension, supported exclusively by the described sequence of declarations and actions.

The proposed financial plan for the 8,500 m metropolitan railway (total cost L. 12,000,000) assigns contributions as follows:
- The State is to cover the majority, classifying the line as 4th Category under national rail law;  
- The Province, the consortia of the secondary lines (Rome–Viterbo and Rome–Marino–Albano), and the city gas company should each make small contributions;  
- The Municipality of Rome is to cede land areas, coordinate building codes, and carry out works in agreement with the railway, but pay no direct cash except for the double-use Tevere bridge and decorative elements.

Given that the line serves three main beneficiaries—the Municipality (urban mobility), the Province and adjacent communes (regional access), and the State (national capital enhancement and military logistics)—critically evaluate the equity of this funding distribution. Then propose one adjustment to the contribution formula that better aligns stakeholder payments with their respective benefits, drawing only on the interests and arguments as presented.

Still in the same barycentric framework of the fixed tetrahedron with vertices e₁,e₂,e₃,e₄ and barycentric coordinates (a₁,…,a₄), the third-degree surface f is defined by the vanishing cubic
   h·a₁·a₂·a₃  +  g·a₁·a₂·a₄  +  f·a₁·a₃·a₄  +  e·a₂·a₃·a₄  =  0.
Define the four regions in (a₁,…,a₄)–space by the sign pattern of the aᵢ:  region A where all four aᵢ>0;  region B where exactly one aᵢ<0;  region C where exactly two aᵢ<0;  region D where exactly three aᵢ<0.  (Points with more than three negative aᵢ cannot satisfy σ>0 and so lie outside the affine tetrahedron.)

Show by sign‐analysis of the four monomials in the cubic that:
  • In region A the equation has no interior solution, and f meets region A only along the six edges of the tetrahedron;
  • In region B there are four disjoint 2-dimensional ‘patches’ of the surface, one in each copy of region B adjoining the tetrahedron;
  • In region C there are six such surface patches;
  • In region D no real surface appears.

Explain how the convexity or concavity of the quadrilateral formed by the four foot‐points X₁,…,X₄ corresponds to these sign‐regions.

A west-east inclined tunnel is driven from a valley wall into a folded limestone mountain. The tunnel section records:  
• 85 ft of mineralized, crushed limestone;  
• 290 ft of shale;  
• 935 ft of mineralized limestone;  
• 30 ft of shale;  
• 50 ft of mineralized limestone;  
• 460 ft of shale;  
• then stratified limestone with bedding dipping northeast.  

Geological mapping shows three shale belts exposed on the eastern slope, only the topmost of which is traced to the surface on the western side of the ridge. Propose a methodological framework to:  
1) correlate each of the three shale intervals encountered underground with the surface shale exposures;  
2) reconstruct the subsurface geometry of the anticline and explain the variable dips observed for each interval;  
3) assess uncertainties and potential miscorrelations arising from local faulting and erosion.  

Answer with a step-by-step approach used by a field geologist with no additional data beyond this tunnel log and surface map.

Context:

A report on preventing wars through enforceable arbitration outlines various "sanctions" to punish states refusing legal settlement. It categorizes these sanctions into five types:
  1) Moral (ecclesiastical excommunication; public honor);
  2) Civil or economic (customs embargoes; asset sequestration; boycotts);
  3) Penal (criminal proceedings against individual officials);
  4) Political (exclusion from international unions; suspension of diplomatic relations; blockades);
  5) Preventive legislative measures (domestic boycott statutes enacted in peace time).

The report asserts that to be effective, sanctions must be both "collective" (agreed among multiple states) and "peaceful". It then argues that economic sanctions—especially national laws of boycottage—best satisfy both conditions today.

Question:
Construct an analytical framework (e.g., a 2×2 matrix or a set of logical criteria) that explains why among these five categories only economic sanctions can currently be both collective and non‐violent. Clearly define your axes or criteria, place each sanction category within your framework, and provide a brief justification for each placement based solely on the reasoning and historical examples given.

Several classical Roman private law actions are said to belong to the group called "in bonum et aequum conceptae": the actio injuriarum, the actio sepulchri violati, the actio de effusis et ejectis (when accidental spillage caused injury), and, according to one scholar, the actio rei uxoriae (the wife’s action for restitution of her dowry upon divorce).  

Each of these first three actions is universally recognized as penal: the praetor’s formula authorized the judge to condemn the defendant to pay "quantum ob eam rem aequum videbitur," an equity‐based, arbitrarily assessed amende_PRIVÉE (pœna privata), with only a maximum set by statute in some cases.  Given this established penal character, explain how the parallels in form and function between actio rei uxoriae and these three penal remedies support the hypothesis that actio rei uxoriae itself was originally a private penal action.  In your answer, analyze:

•  The role of the formulaic terms (e.g., "quod aequius melius erit" and the mandate to arbitrate the penalty) in revealing a punitive origin;

•  The doctrinal significance of grouping actio rei uxoriae with actions that permit judicial discretion to inflict a pœna privata;

•  Any limits or caveats in this analogy (for example, later civil evolution of remedies) and how they weigh upon the overall argument.

Build your reasoning solely on the arguments and textual evidence cited above.

In mid-Republican Rome, a body of formal actions (legis actiones) governed all private suits before a magistrate, rigidly requiring the precise words and ceremonial steps of statutes such as the Twelve Tables. Around the same time, scholars report that a law known as the lex Aebutia empowered the praetor to dispense with those formal actions and substitute a brief written summary (formula) of the dispute, even when it conflicted with an existing statute. Yet many modern writers maintain that the lex Aebutia was merely a procedural reform, not a grant of substantive law-making power to the praetor.  

Drawing exclusively on the evidence and arguments assembled in surviving sources (notably Gaius’ commentaries and an anecdote in Gellius highlighting that decemviral institutions like the talio and the old usury limit were “sopite” by the lex Aebutia), construct a rigorous analytical framework for evaluating whether a single statute is “purely procedural” or also “material” in effect.  

Your answer should:
1. Identify at least three distinct criteria by which one can judge whether a reform altered only process or also substantive rights;
2. Show how each criterion applies to the lex Aebutia as described in the ancient testimony;
3. Draw a reasoned conclusion, based on those criteria, about the true nature of the lex Aebutia.

An anecdote in Aulus Gellius recounts that, when asked about archaic Roman remedies—proletarii, adsidui, subvades, the 25-asse penalty, the lex talionis (‘eye for an eye’), and the mixed-rope / greensack procedure for furtum—the jurist Labeo replied that he could not speak of those because they had fallen into oblivion (‘consopitae’) by virtue of a single statute called the lex Aebutia.  

Given only this effect—that numerous venerable institutions were abolished simultaneously—undertake a methodical reconstruction of the lex Aebutia’s probable substantive provisions.  Specifically:
1. Identify at least three distinct types of clauses (by title or rubric) that lex Aebutia must have contained to produce the simultaneous suppression of all these decemviral remedies;
2. For each clause, explain the logical inference from the stated effects to that clause’s existence;
3. Discuss how this hypothetical reconstruction fits the broader description of lex Aebutia as both procedural (introducing formulary litigation) and material (creating or abolishing substantive rights).

Roman private-law legislation virtually ceased after the mid-third century BC, only to be resumed under Augustus. Meanwhile, the praetorian edict—yearly proclamations of an official endowed with iurisdictio—grew into a comprehensive body of law, amended annually by successive praetors and finally consolidated under Hadrian.  

Assuming that after lex Aebutia (c. 2nd century BC) the assemblies no longer enacted private-law statutes, develop a systematic dating method to divide the extant provisions of the Hadrianic edict (as preserved by Salvius Julianus) into two broad chronological cohorts:
  1. Edictal norms that modify or abrogate earlier statutory provisions (thus post-lex Aebutia, roughly 6th–8th century of Rome);  
  2. Edictal norms that govern new matters or reflect practices predating lex Aebutia (thus likely pre-6th century).  

Your answer should:
•  Specify at least three distinct criteria (philological, substantive, procedural) to assign a provision to one cohort or the other;  
•  Illustrate each criterion with an example drawn from Salvius Julianus’ redaction (e.g., mention a class of action that ‘‘imitates ius legitimum’’ versus one ‘‘created out of the praetor’s potestas’’);  
•  Discuss potential sources of error or ambiguity in this dating method and how to mitigate them.
